From c95e136a945d9858f0a71d1467328f19b8ddd031 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 16:01:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 32/61] nouveau/vk: nvkmd_horizon, check all three ways a push
 truncates
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

nvkmd_horizon_ctx_exec already refuses a push whose byte count is not a
multiple of four, with a comment saying why: a GPFIFO entry counts
dwords, and a size that is not a whole number of them silently loses
the tail, which the GPU reports much later as a channel fault with no
obvious cause.

That reasoning covers two more cases the check did not. An entry
carries its address in GP_ENTRY0_GET | ENTRY1_GET_HI with the low two
bits unavailable, and its length as a 21-bit dword count (envytools
gf100+ GPFIFO entry format). So a misaligned address loses its low bits
and a push of 2^21 dwords or more wraps its count -- the same
truncation, through two other doors.

The nouveau backend asserts both (nvkmd_nouveau_ctx.c:198-199). An
assert is enough there because those values go on to a kernel that
validates them; here they go straight into an entry whose fields just
truncate, so they are refused rather than asserted.

Neither is reachable from NVK today: push memory is dword-aligned by
construction and a command buffer's chunks are far below 8 MiB. This
closes the class rather than the instance.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 4 item 7 (submit)
Why: nvkmd hands the backend a byte address and a byte count, while a
  GPFIFO entry has a dword-aligned address field and a 21-bit dword
  count, and the narrowing is silent.
Evidence: nvkmd_nouveau_ctx.c:198-199 asserts (addr % 4) == 0 and
  size_B < (1u << 23) for the same values, calling it "the hardware
  limit on all current GPUs".
Upstream: no — this is the Horizon backend's own submit path.
---
 .../vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c  | 31 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 31 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c
index f511a98..af736e3 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c
@@ -135,6 +135,37 @@ nvkmd_horizon_ctx_exec(struct nvkmd_ctx *_ctx,
                           i, execs[i].size_B);
       }
 
+      /* The other two ways the same truncation happens, which the check
+       * above alone does not catch. Both are asserts in the nouveau
+       * backend (nvkmd_nouveau_ctx.c:198-199), where an assert is enough
+       * because that path hands the values to a kernel that validates
+       * them; here they go into a GPFIFO entry whose fields simply
+       * truncate.
+       *
+       * A GPFIFO entry carries the address in GP_ENTRY0_GET | ENTRY1_GET_HI
+       * with the low two bits unavailable, and the length as a 21-bit
+       * dword count (envytools gf100+ GPFIFO entry format). So an address
+       * that is not dword-aligned loses its low bits, and a push of 2^21
+       * dwords or more wraps its count — the GPU then executes a stream
+       * that ends mid-method and reports it much later as a channel fault
+       * with no obvious cause. That is exactly the failure this check
+       * exists to prevent, so it should cover all three doors, not one.
+       */
+      if (execs[i].addr % 4 != 0) {
+         STACK_ARRAY_FINISH(spans);
+         return vk_errorf(log_obj, VK_ERROR_UNKNOWN,
+                          "nvkmd_horizon: push %" PRIu32 " is at 0x%" PRIx64
+                          ", which is not dword-aligned", i, execs[i].addr);
+      }
+
+      if (execs[i].size_B >= (UINT32_C(1) << 23)) {
+         STACK_ARRAY_FINISH(spans);
+         return vk_errorf(log_obj, VK_ERROR_UNKNOWN,
+                          "nvkmd_horizon: push %" PRIu32 " is %" PRIu32
+                          " bytes, over the 8 MiB a GPFIFO entry's 21-bit "
+                          "dword count can express", i, execs[i].size_B);
+      }
+
       spans[i].gpu_va = execs[i].addr;
       spans[i].num_dwords = execs[i].size_B / 4;
    }
